            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northeast James Bay",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The marine climate in northeast James Bay changed abruptly in the late 1990s. Coastal Eeyou have remarked on the way climate change is transforming coastal ecosystems and mention, in particular, loss of sea ice45. Climate scientists detect a transition the mid-1990s after 1996 from a relatively stable regime of seasonal ice and surface-water temperature extremes in the broader Hudson Bay region to much greater fluctuations from year to year52. In northeast James Bay, due in part to a strong El Ni\u00f1o, the winter of 1997/1998 was exceptionally warm with low snow cover. Warm air temperatures continued into spring and the regional sea ice broke up in mid- May of 1998 about one month earlier than the mean breakup date during 1982-1997 (Figure 4B).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
